    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "03b839f4_06ad0a52",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 1258954
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-02T23:28:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The GetPrimitiveArrayCritical()/ReleasePrimitiveArrayCritical() is basically a critical section blocking GC from passing a very important phase and hence making progress. So if this critical-section can be long (as suggested in the commit message) then this CL is doing the right thing.\n\nAdded Hans, in case he wants to add more insights.",
      "parentUuid": "856babc7_b74fd099",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e7eb8c37_a5acdd35",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003224
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-02T23:40:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "my question was \"is an extra allocation and GetByteArray _Region_ () really required, or would just using GetByteArray _Elements_ () be better?\". it requires a pin for that array, but doesn\u0027t block the whole GC...",
      "parentUuid": "03b839f4_06ad0a52",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed65e53a_50795e90",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 1258954
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-03T00:06:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry, my bad.\n\nI looked at the code of GetByteArrayElements(). It makes a copy if the byte-array is movable. Otherwise not.\n\nIn any case, I agree GetByteArrayElements() seems like a better choice than GetByteArrayRegion(), unless `buffer` is a big movable array and `length` is expected to be small.",
      "parentUuid": "e7eb8c37_a5acdd35",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "af8a54a2_8f1e7503",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 3404192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-03T03:32:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m happy to make this change, but I\u0027d like to explain the thought process behind using Get/Set ByteArrayRegion:\n\nIt seems to me that Get/Set ByteArrayRegion is a better choice if the array is movable. Since I only need to do one of Get/Set ByteArrayRegion I only need 1 copy. With Get/Release ByteArrayElements I need to call both Get and Release, and each does a copy. So that\u0027s 1 copy vs 2, and the copies are potentially much smaller if we\u0027re operating on a sub-array of the array.\n\nThere is an optimization I can apply when the direction is USB_DIR_OUT: I can pass JNI_ABORT to ReleaseByteArrayElements and thereby avoid the second copy. But I can\u0027t do any such optimization for USB_DIR_IN - the second copy is unavoidable. And the copies will be of the entire array even if we\u0027re only operating on a sub-array.\n\nGet/Release ByteArrayElements is probably a better choice if the the array is non-movable, but I don\u0027t think it\u0027s a good idea to bet on this being the case. It\u0027s hard for me to exactly reason about how likely objects are to be movable, but I do see that large objects get allocated non-movable (i.e. in a discontinuous space): https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:art/runtime/gc/space/large_object_space.h;l\u003d42 and the large object threshold seems to be 12KB by default: https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:art/runtime/gc/heap.h;l\u003d155 so I believe that small arrays (\u003c12KB) are possibly (even likely?) movable.\n\nGiven that movable arrays are quite possible, I think we should choose operations that work well with both movable and non-movable arrays. An implementation that uses Get/Release ByteArrayElements would have exceptionally poor performance if someone passed in a small sub-array of a largish array (close to but not above the large object threshold). Even if we\u0027re operating on the entire array, it\u0027s not good to do 2 copies instead of 1. I expect passing in sub-arrays is probably unusual, but the API explicitly allows it, so I think that would be an unfortunate performance foot-gun.\n\nLet me know what you\u0027d prefer. I\u0027m perfectly happy to do either. If we do go with Get/Release ByteArrayElements here, we should probably also consider doing the same for bulkTransfer. In https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/platform/frameworks/base/+/3056082 I made it use Get/Set ByteArrayRegion.",
      "parentUuid": "ed65e53a_50795e90",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04c19ed6_2a5590fa",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-03T18:59:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess we have no real idea of what fraction of the array we\u0027re usually interested in, and what typical length values are?\n\nIIUC, for output of (nearly) the entire array GetByteArrayElements followed by JNI_ABORT when you release the buffer, is probably better. In all other cases, what you have looks at least competitive to me. I don\u0027t know enough about the context to tell whether that\u0027s worth optimizing.",
      "parentUuid": "af8a54a2_8f1e7503",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84f63810_a90dbaad",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003224
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-03T19:01:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "my bias is that this means that every transfer requires a _native_ heap allocation and free :-) but, yeah, without stats it\u0027s hard to know.\n\nsince this is the same as the other existing code, i\u0027ll +2 as \"better than what\u0027s currently there\", even if it\u0027s possible it\u0027s not the optimal choice.",
      "parentUuid": "04c19ed6_2a5590fa",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0dc13a31_053c6db3",
        "filename": "core/jni/android_hardware_UsbDeviceConnection.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 169,
      "author": {
        "id": 3404192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-04T01:45:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks @enh@google.com. Again, I\u0027m more than happy to change to whatever you like. Just say what you\u0027d prefer and I\u0027ll make the change.\n\nMy opinion: I believe the only cases where we\u0027d avoid the native heap allocation/free are where the buffer is greater than the large object threshold (12k bytes by default), and I suspect that an extra copy of 12k bytes is quite a bit worse. The JNI_ABORT optimization does muddy the waters a bit. It really would be better when the direction is USB_DIR_OUT, the array is large, and no sub-array is specified, but I don\u0027t think it\u0027s worth the complexity to special case that.",
      "parentUuid": "84f63810_a90dbaad",
      "revId": "78033b77795fd5d0d56d56a973543c3993af3e76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
